<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Adid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1171">1171</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Adid was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, who died in 1171. During his reign real power passed to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a>, culminating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who held the vizierate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Saladin ended Fatimid rule and restored recognition of the Abbasid caliph around the time of al-Adid's death, opening the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Adid was the son of the Fatimid prince Yusuf, a younger son of the eleventh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hafiz. According to the commonly accepted date he was born on 9 May 1151, though some authors give 1145 or 1149. In 1154 the vizier Abbas ibn Abi'l-Futuh had Yusuf executed after the assassination of the caliph al-Zafir. By then the caliphs had become virtual puppets of their chief ministers, whose names were mentioned beside theirs in the Friday sermon, and the dynasty's Isma'ili doctrine had lost much of its appeal. Al-Adid was raised to the throne on 23 July 1160, aged nine, by the vizier Tala'i ibn Ruzzik, who married him to one of his daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Throughout his reign al-Adid was a figurehead while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> fought for the spoils. Ibn Ruzzik was assassinated on 11 September 1161 and followed by his son Ruzzik ibn Tala'i. Shawar overthrew Ruzzik in December 1162, and was himself evicted by Dirgham in August 1163. The turmoil allowed the Crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Zengid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> to intervene. Shawar returned with Nur al-Din's general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> and Shirkuh's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, then allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> against them. In 1167 a Crusader garrison was placed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and in 1168 King Amalric besieged the city. Shirkuh arrived in January 1169, had Shawar executed and became vizier. When Shirkuh died on 23 March 1169, Saladin was appointed in his place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> gradually dismantled the Fatimid regime. He purged Fatimid troops, defeating a revolt of the Black African troops on 21 August 1169, and in August 1170 he changed the call to prayer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> form. He placed Sunni jurists in office and suspended Isma'ili lectures at the al-Azhar Mosque. On 10 September 1171 the name of the Abbasid caliph al-Mustadi was proclaimed in the sermon in place of al-Adid's. Al-Adid died on 13 September 1171, aged twenty, probably of a severe illness; some medieval sources claim poison or murder, but the historian Heinz Halm finds no serious evidence for it. The Fatimid family was placed under house arrest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Isma'ilism</a> had been effectively purged from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by the end of the thirteenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Adid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Two years later, as the last Fatimid caliph <b>al-Adid</b> lay dying in September 1171, Saladin had the Friday sermon pronounced in the name of the Abbasid caliph of Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The last caliph, <b>al-Adid</b>, died on 13 September 1171, reportedly unaware that his caliphate had already ceased to exist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Zengids were Sunni, and Shirkuh and Saladin held Egypt as Fatimid viziers in Nur ad-Din's name until Saladin, after <b>al-Adid</b>'s death in 1171, switched Egypt to the Abbasid caliph.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1161" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of al-Adid"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1161">What was here in 1161? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
